<title>GERMANY: German steel alliance to cost 8,000 jobs-union.</title>
<headline>German steel alliance to cost 8,000 jobs-union.</headline>
<dateline>FRANKFURT 1997-03-25</dateline>
<text>
<p>The planned alliance between German steel firms Krupp AG and Thyssen AG will cost as many as 8,000 jobs over four to five years, Germany's IG Metall trade union said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>Harald Schartau, head of IG Metall's North Rhine Westphalia unit, told Reuters late on Tuesday the union would undertake widespread industrial action to protest against the companies' plans to co-operate.</p>
<p>Schartau, speaking after works councils and unions were informed of the steel firms' plans, said the companies also planned to shut down a Krupp plant in Dortmund.</p>
<p>IG Metall would show &quot;massive resistance&quot; over the next four weeks, he added. Krupp and Thyssen want to use this period to finalise plans for a joint steel venture.</p>
<p>Krupp and Thyssen agreed to explore ways to co-operate on Monday, thus rendering void Krupp's takeover bid for its rival, which it launched last week.</p>
<p>--Frankfurt Newsroom, +49 69 756525</p>
